        <div class="card-body">
          <h5 class="card-title">Project 1</h5>
          <p class="card-text">Project description.</p>
          <button class="btn btn-primary"
                  data-bs-toggle="modal"
                  data-bs-target="#project1Modal">
            View Details
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

Key points:
- `data-src` holds the real image URL.
- `src="placeholder.jpg"` is a small low-resolution placeholder to avoid layout shifts.
- Add a consistent lazy class such as `lazy` if your config uses `lazyClass: 'lazy'`, or use the default `lazyload`.
- Keep meaningful `alt` text for SEO/accessibility.
- Use `data-bs-toggle` and `data-bs-target` for Bootstrap 5 modals, not the old `data-toggle`/`data-target`.

### 3. Configure lazysizes and add error handling
Use your existing class setup and add an error class:
```javascript
import 'lazysizes';
import 'lazysizes/plugins/parent-fit/ls.parent-fit';

const lazyLoadInstance = lazysizes.config({
  loadingClass: 'ls-loading',
  loadedClass: 'ls-loaded',
  lazyClass: 'lazy',
  errorClass: 'ls-error'
});

const galleryImages = document.querySelectorAll('.gallery-image');
galleryImages.forEach((image) => {
  image.classList.add('lazy');
  image.setAttribute('data-src', image.dataset.src || image.src);
  image.src = 'placeholder.jpg';
  image.removeAttribute('srcset'); // prevents premature loading

  image.addEventListener('load', () => {
    console.log(`${image.src} loaded successfully.`);
  });

  image.addEventListener('error', () => {
    console.error(`Failed to load ${image.src}.`);
    image.src = 'fallback.jpg';
    image.classList.remove('ls-error');
  });
});

lazyLoadInstance.revalidate();

document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  lazyLoadInstance.revalidate();
});
```

### 4. If cards/modals are rendered dynamically
After appending new cards or modals with JavaScript, run the same lazy-load setup and call `revalidate()` again so lazysizes picks up the new `.gallery-image` elements.

### 5. For modal images, defer until the modal opens
Since your gallery uses project-detail modals, keep modal image URLs in `data-src` and only swap them in when the Bootstrap modal is shown. This helps avoid the synchronous image-loading delay you identified in the modal popup:
```javascript
modalElement.addEventListener('shown.bs.modal', () => {
  const img = modalElement.querySelector('img[data-src]');
  if (!img) return;

  const loader = new Image();
  loader.onload = () => {
    img.src = img.dataset.src;
  };
  loader.onerror = () => {
    img.src = 'fallback.jpg';
  };
  loader.src = img.dataset.src;
});
```

### 6. Bootstrap/CSS integration
- Use Bootstrap’s `card-img-top`, `w-100`, and grid classes.
- Keep card images responsive with `width: 100%; height: auto; object-fit: cover;`.
- Namespace custom classes, e.g. `.cb-project-card`, to avoid conflicts with Bootstrap.
- If you want the absolute simplest approach, native `<img loading="lazy">` is the easiest option; but for your existing lazysizes setup, error handling, fallback images, and modal deferral, the lazysizes approach above is the better fit.
